  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fostering the Economic and Social Well-being of Waste Workers</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Coca-Cola shared its World Without Waste vision in 2018 which committed to an ambitious goal to collect and recycle the equivalent volume of bottle/can it sells by 2030 reaching a 100% collection and recycling rate of all its packaging. Cordaid aims to contribute to SDG 12, Responsible Consumption and Production by supporting this initiative of Coca-Cola by dual approaches: by contributing to the wellbeing (decent work) of the informal waste workers and through capacity building of the informal waste collectors and associations for sustainable and effective management of the plastic waste and PET bottles collection (substantially reduce waste generation through recycling) and supply to the recycling partner of Coca-Cola. 
Cordaid, an international organization, works across 20 countries in Asia, Africa, Latin America, and the Middle East with a record of accomplishment of more than 50 years globally and about 49 years in Bangladesh. Programs have reached more than 1.3 million people in the country in the last 5 years. Since 1st of January 2021, ICCO merged with Cordaid, a Dutch organization to increase its impact in addressing the global challenge by combining networks, knowledge, and organizational infrastructure. ICCO has had a strong field presence in the Rohingya crisis since the beginning of the influx in 2017. In 2020, ICCO started the Circular Food Assistance project with WFP, which is the first-ever example around the world to upcycle its own produced food packaging waste into usable products, provide skill development and income opportunities to the Rohingya refugees which enhances their daily lives, while creating a cleaner camp environment. ICCO used the Human-Centered Design (HCD) approach to design more inclusive programme interventions that give ownership, dignity, and pride to the users. For our initiatives on Solid Waste Management, ICCO received the &#x2018;Bangladesh Innovation Award&#x2019; under the product development category and Sprint Global Award from the WFP Innovation Accelerator programme in 2020. 
Cordaid realizes there are collaboration opportunities with Coca-Cola where Cordaid can bring in its expertise on solid waste and enterprise promotion to support the World Without Waste vision by improving the skills &amp; livelihood of the waste workers which aligns with the global TOC of Cordaid.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fostering economic and social well-being of waste workers for safe and dignified collection of Plastic Waste for recycling.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Total number of beneficiaries are 420 (400 waste collectors and 20 waste aggregators) in Dhaka North City Corporation (DNCC) and Narayanganj City Corporation (NCC)</narrative>
  </description>
